//
// A WRITE THROUGH ANY MEMBER OF A LINKED GROUP REACHES EVERY MEMBER (`@FR-Col-Group`).  Two or more
// collections over one element type in one struct are routes to a single record set (loft#843), so
// whatever spells the write reaches all of them: a whole vector value written into a member
// (loft#1152), a fill through any member of any kind (loft#901), and an element-level write through
// the vector member — the record leaves every keyed sibling before it is replaced, nulled or
// removed, and is indexed again under the key it now carries — as a removal through a keyed member
// or `e#remove` already unlinked it (loft#900, loft#903).  Three sections, each with its cells and
// receipt.
//
// ── 1152-a-vector-value-into-a-group-reaches-every-member ──
//
// A whole VECTOR VALUE written into a linked-group member reaches every member.
//
// Two or more collections over one element type in one struct are auto-linked into
// several routes to a SINGLE record set (loft#843).  `Stores::record_finish` is what keeps
// them agreeing: it walks the field's `other_indexes` and inserts the record into every
// sibling, and every route that adds records ONE AT A TIME reaches it.  A whole-vector
// write does not — `OpAppendVector` reaches `vector_add` → `vector_add_array`, which moves
// the records in bulk — so `s.v = rows()` and `s.v += rows()` filled the vector and left
// every sibling view EMPTY.
//
// Nothing said so, on either backend: `len` of the view answered `0` and a lookup answered
// `null`, and both are legal values for a group that happens to be empty.  That is the
// state DATABASE.md says has no repair — *"nothing repairs an index that silently does not
// index its records"* — which is also why the cure is not to refuse the spelling.
//
// ⚠ **`len` is the wrong oracle and `ce_identity` is why.**  A group is several routes to
// ONE record set, not a copy per member.  A fix that gave each view its own copy would make
// every length cell here read correct while `ce_identity` — mutate through the vector, read
// through the view — still answered the OLD value.  `ce_by_key` is the second half: the
// view must hold the right record under the right key, which a count cannot witness either.
//
// ⚠ **`cc_accumulate` scores against the ELEMENT-WISE spelling rather than a hand-computed
// number.**  Element-by-element is the route that already worked and is the workaround the
// issue published, so it is the reference behaviour; the requirement is AGREEMENT, which is
// what makes the duplicate-key case (`+=` the same rows twice) checkable at all without
// deciding here what a keyed view should do with a repeated key.
//
// ⚠ **`cf_two_vectors` is the CONTROL that carries the scope.**  Two `vector` fields over one
// element type never link — inserting into one must not propagate to the other — and that is
// what keeps "at least one member is keyed" the rule rather than "the contents match".
// `cf_vector_second` is the same whole-value route spelled through a group whose vector is
// declared SECOND; it read `0` while group FORMATION still depended on declaration order,
// which was a separate defect in a separate place (@FR-Col-Group, loft#1158, guarded by
// `a-groups-members-are-found-by-their-element-type-whatever-the-order-or-the-nullability.loft`).
//
// @falsified-at: f26b708a — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.
//
// ── 901-linked-group-fill ──
//
// Filling a linked collection group reaches every member, whichever member it is
// spelled through and whatever kinds the members are.
//
// Two or more collections over one element type in one struct are auto-linked into
// several routes to a SINGLE record set (`Field.other_indexes`, loft#843). Three
// pair shapes never got the second route: `hash` + `index` kept ONE element however
// many went in, `hash` + `hash` built the right NUMBER of entries all naming the
// first record, and `sorted` + `sorted` / `vector` + `sorted` stayed empty. No
// diagnostic — a lookup through the second route simply missed, so a smoke test with
// a single element passed. Identical on both backends and on the published 2026.8.0.
//
// One fact explains all of them: every member names its elements by a 4-byte record
// id. A hash slot encodes `rec.rec` (`hash::SLOT_RECORD`), an `array` / `ordered`
// slot stores it raw and reads it back at a hard-coded payload start, and an `index`
// keeps its red-black links in FIELDS of the record. None can express a position
// INSIDE a record, so an element that does not own one is unaddressable through its
// siblings — and two shapes made elements that do not: a hash packs its entries into
// a shared chunk arena, and a `sorted` stores its elements inline.
//
// `record_new` already refuses the arena for an element type flagged `linked`, and
// `finish_type` already promotes `vector` → `array` and `sorted` → `ordered` for one.
// The flag was only ever set as a SIDE EFFECT of that promotion, so a group whose
// members are all keyed never set it. It is now set for every group element type.
//
// The cells check CONTENTS, not just length: `hash` + `hash` had the right length
// with every entry naming the same record, which is exactly what a length cannot
// see.
//
// **Every row has its OWN element type, and that is what makes this file bite.**
// The promotion is keyed on the element type across the WHOLE program, so one
// `vector<E>` beside one `hash<E[k]>` anywhere makes every other collection over `E`
// record-backed and every row below passes on the unfixed build. Written over a
// shared `E` this file printed `901 ok` on the published 2026.8.0 — a guard that
// could not fail. Rows a1/a4 are the two ends of that: a1 has no vector or sorted
// member at all, a4 has no hash or index one.
//
// The controls carry the scope, and the boundary is @FR-Col-Group: a group needs at
// least one KEYED member, and nothing else about how it is written matters. c1 pins a
// plain `vector` as the SECOND field, which IS a member — the pairing is a fact about
// the PAIR, not about which field is being declared. It used to read 0 here and this
// file pinned that as the scope, exactly as it once did for c2: the group formed off
// the keyed field being ADDED, so `{ a: hash<E[k]>, b: vector<E> }` built two
// independent collections while `{ b: vector<E>, a: hash<E[k]> }` built one record
// set, in both fill directions, with nothing saying which you had. c4 is the boundary
// the old c1 comment was reaching for and did not test: two plain VECTORS over one
// element type must stay independent, or inserting into one would alias the other.
// c2 is the same story a kind earlier: a `trie` second field IS keyed, so it joins the
// group — it also used to read 0 here, and that was the defect, not the boundary
// (loft#927). c3 pins a LONE hash, which must keep packing its entries into the arena
// (@PLN135 arc H): a fix that made every hash allocate one record per entry would pass
// every row above and silently give back that plan's win.
//
// ── a-group-element-written-through-the-vector-member-reaches-every-member ──
//
// An element-level write through the VECTOR member of a linked collection group acts on the
// group: the record leaves every keyed sibling before it is replaced, nulled or removed, and
// a replaced record is indexed again under the key it now carries.
//
// @FR-Col-Group — two or more collections over one element type in one struct are several
// ROUTES to a single record set, a record entering through any member is in every member and
// a record leaving through any member leaves every member, by any write route.  Every route
// that ADDS a record reaches `Stores::record_finish`, the per-record chokepoint that keeps the
// members agreeing, and every removal spelled through a KEYED member or as `e#remove` emits
// the unlinks (loft#900, loft#903).  The element-level writes through the vector member
// reached neither: `w.es[0] = E{k:11}` copied into the record IN PLACE, so `by_k` went on
// holding it under the hash of the OLD key — `by_k[11]` null, `by_k[7]` null, `len(by_k)`
// still 2; `w.es[0] = null` on a `vector<E?>` left the view one entry too long; and
// `w.es.remove(0)` left the removed key findable, so a re-add of that key counted twice.
// Silent on both backends, the same one nesting level down.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  the WRITE (replace by literal, replace by local, null, replace into a
//                        null slot, `remove(i)`, `remove` in a loop, `remove` through a `&`
//                        parameter), the holder's element (dense `vector<E>`, nullable
//                        `vector<E?>`), the DEPTH (a top-level struct, a struct that is a
//                        vector element, one that is a `vector<R?>` element), the declaring
//                        type (struct, enum VARIANT), the member COUNT (2, 4 — hash, sorted
//                        and trie re-linked together), a replace with the SAME key, an index
//                        expression with a side effect, and a group with TWO vector members.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   `hash` is the keyed kind everywhere but r9; `index` and `spatial`
//                        take the same `insert_record` / `remove` arms the clear and the
//                        keyed removal already exercise for every kind (898, 900).  Removal
//                        spelled through a KEYED member is 900's; `e#remove` is 903's and
//                        appears here only as a control.
//
// **Every row has its OWN element type.**  The record-backed promotion is keyed on the
// ELEMENT TYPE across the whole program (the 901 / 1158 lesson).
//
// @falsified-at: 2b992851 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  Every row asserts, and
// an assert stops the run, so the count is one: on the control r1 fires first (`by_k[11]` reads
// null with `len(by_k)` still 2); r2-r18 are broken there too, each on its own binary run.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see GUARDS.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on
//   both trees, measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 2b992851; falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The exit channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not scored in that
//   run.

// ---- r1: replace by index write, dense holder ---------------------------------------------

struct Cg1152V  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text, cg_v: integer }
struct Cg1152H  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152I  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152T  { cg_n: text, cg_k: integer }
struct Cg1152S  { cg_k: integer, cg_k2: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152M  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152N  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152A  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }
struct Cg1152E  { cg_k: integer, cg_n: text }

struct CgVec   { cg_d: vector<Cg1152V>, cg_look: sorted<Cg1152V[cg_k]> }
struct CgHash  { cg_d: vector<Cg1152H>, cg_look: hash<Cg1152H[cg_k]> }
struct CgIdx   { cg_d: vector<Cg1152I>, cg_look: index<Cg1152I[cg_k]> }
struct CgTrie  { cg_d: vector<Cg1152T>, cg_look: trie<Cg1152T[cg_n]> }
struct CgSpat  { cg_d: vector<Cg1152S>, cg_look: spatial<Cg1152S[cg_k, cg_k2]> }
struct CgThree { cg_d: vector<Cg1152M>, cg_byk: sorted<Cg1152M[cg_k]>, cg_byn: hash<Cg1152M[cg_n]> }
struct CgNull  { cg_d: vector<Cg1152N?>, cg_look: hash<Cg1152N[cg_k]> }
struct CgTwoV  { cg_a: vector<Cg1152A>, cg_b: vector<Cg1152A> }
struct CgVSec  { cg_look: sorted<Cg1152E[cg_k]>, cg_d: vector<Cg1152E> }

enum CgEnum { CgNone, CgSome { cg_ea: vector<Cg1152M>, cg_eb: sorted<Cg1152M[cg_k]> } }

fn cg_rows()  -> vector<Cg1152V> { [Cg1152V { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven", cg_v: 70 }, Cg1152V { cg_k: 8, cg_n: "eight", cg_v: 80 }] }
fn cg_rowsh() -> vector<Cg1152H> { [Cg1152H { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven" }, Cg1152H { cg_k: 8, cg_n: "eight" }] }
fn cg_rowsi() -> vector<Cg1152I> { [Cg1152I { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven" }, Cg1152I { cg_k: 8, cg_n: "eight" }] }
fn cg_rowst() -> vector<Cg1152T> { [Cg1152T { cg_n: "seven", cg_k: 7 }, Cg1152T { cg_n: "eight", cg_k: 8 }] }
fn cg_rowss() -> vector<Cg1152S> { [Cg1152S { cg_k: 1, cg_k2: 2, cg_n: "a" }, Cg1152S { cg_k: 3, cg_k2: 4, cg_n: "b" }] }
fn cg_rowsm() -> vector<Cg1152M> { [Cg1152M { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven" }, Cg1152M { cg_k: 8, cg_n: "eight" }] }
fn cg_rowse() -> vector<Cg1152E> { [Cg1152E { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven" }] }

fn test_a_vector_value_into_a_group_reaches_every_member() {
  // ---- the two spellings the issue reports, on a group that exists ----------------
  cg_a1 = CgVec { }; cg_a1.cg_d = cg_rows();
  assert(len(cg_a1.cg_look) == 2, "assign a vector VALUE; view has {len(cg_a1.cg_look)}");
  cg_a2 = CgVec { }; cg_a2.cg_d += cg_rows();
  assert(len(cg_a2.cg_look) == 2, "append a vector VALUE; view has {len(cg_a2.cg_look)}");

  // ---- the two routes that already worked, which must not move --------------------
  cg_a3 = CgVec { }; for cg_r in cg_rows() { cg_a3.cg_d += [cg_r]; }
  assert(len(cg_a3.cg_look) == 2, "element-wise; view has {len(cg_a3.cg_look)}");
  cg_a4 = CgVec { }; cg_a4.cg_d = [Cg1152V { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven", cg_v: 70 }, Cg1152V { cg_k: 8, cg_n: "eight", cg_v: 80 }];
  assert(len(cg_a4.cg_look) == 2, "element LIST literal; view has {len(cg_a4.cg_look)}");

  // ---- ONE record set, not a copy per member: the oracle a length cannot give -----
  cg_id = CgVec { }; cg_id.cg_d = cg_rows(); cg_id.cg_d[0].cg_v = 999;
  assert((cg_id.cg_look[7].cg_v ?? -1) == 999,
    "ce_identity: a write through the vector is visible through the view; got {cg_id.cg_look[7].cg_v ?? -1}");
  cg_bk = CgVec { }; cg_bk.cg_d = cg_rows();
  assert((cg_bk.cg_look[8].cg_n ?? "ABSENT") == "eight",
    "ce_by_key: the view holds the right record under the right key; got `{cg_bk.cg_look[8].cg_n ?? "ABSENT"}`");

  // ---- every member, not just the first ------------------------------------------
  cg_t3 = CgThree { }; cg_t3.cg_d = cg_rowsm();
  assert(len(cg_t3.cg_byk) == 2 && len(cg_t3.cg_byn) == 2,
    "three members; byk={len(cg_t3.cg_byk)} byn={len(cg_t3.cg_byn)}");

  // ---- every keyed KIND ----------------------------------------------------------
  cg_kh = CgHash { }; cg_kh.cg_d = cg_rowsh();
  assert(len(cg_kh.cg_look) == 2, "hash view has {len(cg_kh.cg_look)}");
  cg_ki = CgIdx  { }; cg_ki.cg_d = cg_rowsi();
  assert(len(cg_ki.cg_look) == 2, "index view has {len(cg_ki.cg_look)}");
  cg_kt = CgTrie { }; cg_kt.cg_d = cg_rowst();
  assert(len(cg_kt.cg_look) == 2, "trie view has {len(cg_kt.cg_look)}");
  cg_ks = CgSpat { }; cg_ks.cg_d = cg_rowss();
  assert(len(cg_ks.cg_look) == 2, "spatial view has {len(cg_ks.cg_look)}");

  // ---- cc_accumulate: the vector-value spelling AGREES with the element-wise one --
  cg_c1 = CgThree { }; cg_c1.cg_d += cg_rowsm(); cg_c1.cg_d += cg_rowsm();
  cg_c2 = CgThree { };
  for cg_x in cg_rowsm() { cg_c2.cg_d += [cg_x]; }
  for cg_y in cg_rowsm() { cg_c2.cg_d += [cg_y]; }
  assert(len(cg_c1.cg_d) == len(cg_c2.cg_d) && len(cg_c1.cg_byk) == len(cg_c2.cg_byk),
    "cc_accumulate: value=({len(cg_c1.cg_d)},{len(cg_c1.cg_byk)}) elementwise=({len(cg_c2.cg_d)},{len(cg_c2.cg_byk)})");

  // ---- replace, empty, self-assign ------------------------------------------------
  cg_r1 = CgVec { }; cg_r1.cg_d = cg_rows(); cg_r1.cg_d = cg_rows();
  assert(len(cg_r1.cg_d) == 2 && len(cg_r1.cg_look) == 2,
    "replace: d={len(cg_r1.cg_d)} view={len(cg_r1.cg_look)}");
  cg_e1 = CgVec { }; cg_e1.cg_d = cg_rows(); cg_e1.cg_d = [];
  assert(len(cg_e1.cg_look) == 0, "empty: view has {len(cg_e1.cg_look)}");
  cg_s1 = CgVec { }; cg_s1.cg_d = cg_rows(); cg_s1.cg_d = cg_s1.cg_d;
  assert(len(cg_s1.cg_d) == 2 && len(cg_s1.cg_look) == 2,
    "self-assign: d={len(cg_s1.cg_d)} view={len(cg_s1.cg_look)}");

  // ---- a NULL element stays in the vector and out of the index (@PLN25 scope B) ---
  cg_n1 = CgNull { }; cg_n1.cg_d = [Cg1152N { cg_k: 7, cg_n: "seven" }, null];
  assert(len(cg_n1.cg_d) == 2 && len(cg_n1.cg_look) == 1,
    "nullable: d={len(cg_n1.cg_d)} view={len(cg_n1.cg_look)} — the null has no key to be found under");

  // ---- an enum VARIANT's fields are a group too, and the guard names the variant --
  cg_v1: CgEnum = CgSome { cg_ea: [], cg_eb: [] };
  cg_v1.cg_ea = cg_rowsm();
  assert(len(cg_v1.cg_eb) == 2,
    "enum variant field; view has {len(cg_v1.cg_eb)} — the variant's own type is what holds the group");

  // ---- CONTROL: two vectors over one element type never link ---------------------
  cg_f1 = CgTwoV { }; cg_f1.cg_a = [Cg1152A { cg_k: 1, cg_n: "one" }];
  assert(len(cg_f1.cg_a) == 1 && len(cg_f1.cg_b) == 0,
    "cf_two_vectors: a={len(cg_f1.cg_a)} b={len(cg_f1.cg_b)} — inserting into one must not reach the other");

  // ---- a plain vector declared AFTER a keyed member is a member too ---------------
  // The whole-value route this file is about, spelled through the member the group used
  // to be blind to: membership is a fact about the PAIR, not about declaration order
  // (@FR-Col-Group, loft#1158).  This cell read `1/0` and was pinned here as the
  // boundary; the order-independence guard is
  // `a-groups-members-are-found-by-their-element-type-whatever-the-order-or-the-nullability.loft`.
  cg_f2 = CgVSec { }; cg_f2.cg_d = cg_rowse();
  assert(len(cg_f2.cg_d) == 1 && len(cg_f2.cg_look) == 1,
    "cf_vector_second: d={len(cg_f2.cg_d)} view={len(cg_f2.cg_look)} — the vector is a member in either order");

  println("1152 ok");
}

struct E1 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E2 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E3 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E4 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E5 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E6 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E7 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E8 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E9 { k: integer, n: text }
struct E10 { k: integer, n: text }

struct HI { a: hash<E1[k]>, b: index<E1[k]> }
struct HH { a: hash<E2[k]>, b: hash<E2[k]> }
struct SS { a: sorted<E3[k]>, b: sorted<E3[k]> }
struct VS { a: vector<E4>, b: sorted<E4[k]> }
struct IH { a: index<E5[k]>, b: hash<E5[k]> }
struct SSS { a: sorted<E6[k]>, b: sorted<E6[k]>, c: sorted<E6[k]> }
struct HV { a: hash<E7[k]>, b: vector<E7> }
struct ST { a: sorted<E8[k]>, b: trie<E8[n]> }
struct LoneH { a: hash<E9[k]>, tag: integer }
struct VV { a: vector<E10>, b: vector<E10> }

// Sum of the keys and of the name lengths over one member — order-independent, and
// it separates "two entries" from "two entries naming one record" (which reads back
// k 1+1 = 2 and n 5+5 = 10 instead of k 1+2 = 3 and n 5+4 = 9).
fn tally(k: integer, n: integer, who: text, want_len: integer, len_got: integer) {
  assert(len_got == want_len, "{who}: len = {len_got}, want {want_len}");
  assert(k == 3, "{who}: key sum = {k}, want 3 (1 + 2)");
  assert(n == 9, "{who}: name bytes = {n}, want 9 (alpha + beta)");
}

fn test_linked_group_fill() {
  // ---- a1: hash + index — the index kept only the first element ----------------
  a1 = HI { a: [], b: [] };
  a1.a = [E1{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E1{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  ka = 0; na = 0; for e1 in a1.a { ka += e1.k; na += len(e1.n); }
  kb = 0; nb = 0; for e1b in a1.b { kb += e1b.k; nb += len(e1b.n); }
  tally(ka, na, "a1 hash", 2, len(a1.a));
  tally(kb, nb, "a1 index", 2, len(a1.b));
  // The lookup is the point of a second route, so pin it rather than the length.
  assert((a1.b[2] ?? E1{k:0,n:""}).n == "beta", "a1: index lookup of key 2 missed");

  // ---- a2: hash + hash — right length, every entry naming the first record ------
  a2 = HH { a: [], b: [] };
  a2.a += [E2{k:1,n:"alpha"}];
  a2.a += [E2{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  ka2 = 0; na2 = 0; for e2 in a2.b { ka2 += e2.k; na2 += len(e2.n); }
  tally(ka2, na2, "a2 second hash", 2, len(a2.b));

  // ---- a3: sorted + sorted — the view was never populated at all ----------------
  a3 = SS { a: [], b: [] };
  a3.a = [E3{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E3{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  ka3 = 0; na3 = 0; for e3 in a3.b { ka3 += e3.k; na3 += len(e3.n); }
  tally(ka3, na3, "a3 second sorted", 2, len(a3.b));

  // ---- a4: vector + sorted — same, and the pairing needs no index anywhere ------
  a4 = VS { a: [], b: [] };
  a4.a = [E4{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E4{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  ka4 = 0; na4 = 0; for e4 in a4.b { ka4 += e4.k; na4 += len(e4.n); }
  tally(ka4, na4, "a4 sorted view", 2, len(a4.b));
  assert((a4.b[1] ?? E4{k:0,n:""}).n == "alpha", "a4: sorted lookup of key 1 missed");

  // ---- b1: spelled through the VIEW — neither member is the privileged one ------
  b1 = IH { a: [], b: [] };
  b1.b = [E5{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E5{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  kb1 = 0; nb1 = 0; for e5 in b1.a { kb1 += e5.k; nb1 += len(e5.n); }
  tally(kb1, nb1, "b1 index reached through the hash", 2, len(b1.a));

  // ---- b2: three members, one fill ---------------------------------------------
  b2 = SSS { a: [], b: [], c: [] };
  b2.a = [E6{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E6{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  kb2 = 0; nb2 = 0; for e6 in b2.b { kb2 += e6.k; nb2 += len(e6.n); }
  kc2 = 0; nc2 = 0; for e6c in b2.c { kc2 += e6c.k; nc2 += len(e6c.n); }
  tally(kb2, nb2, "b2 second sorted", 2, len(b2.b));
  tally(kc2, nc2, "b2 third sorted", 2, len(b2.c));

  // ---- c1: a plain vector as the SECOND field IS a group member ----------------
  // Filled through the KEYED member, and the reverse direction too: the pair is the
  // group, so neither spelling and neither route is the privileged one.
  c1 = HV { a: [], b: [] };
  c1.a = [E7{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E7{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  assert(len(c1.a) == 2, "c1 hash len = {len(c1.a)}, want 2");
  assert(len(c1.b) == 2, "c1 vector len = {len(c1.b)}, want 2 — a vector second is still a member");
  c1b = HV { a: [], b: [] };
  c1b.b = [E7{k:3,n:"gamma"}, E7{k:4,n:"delta"}];
  assert(len(c1b.a) == 2 && len(c1b.b) == 2,
    "c1b filled through the vector: hash={len(c1b.a)} vector={len(c1b.b)}, want 2/2");

  // ---- c2: a trie IS a group member, like every other keyed kind ---------------
  c2 = ST { a: [], b: [] };
  c2.a = [E8{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E8{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  assert(len(c2.a) == 2, "c2 sorted len = {len(c2.a)}, want 2");
  assert(len(c2.b) == 2, "c2 trie len = {len(c2.b)}, want 2 — a trie joins the group");

  // ---- c3: a lone hash keeps its arena — it has no sibling to be addressed by ---
  c3 = LoneH { a: [], tag: 7 };
  c3.a = [E9{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E9{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  kc3 = 0; nc3 = 0; for e9 in c3.a { kc3 += e9.k; nc3 += len(e9.n); }
  tally(kc3, nc3, "c3 lone hash", 2, len(c3.a));
  assert(c3.tag == 7, "c3 tag = {c3.tag}, want 7");

  // ---- c4: two plain VECTORS are not a group — the boundary c1 is not -----------
  // Neither member is keyed, so there is no group and inserting into one must not
  // reach the other.  This is what "widening the pairing test" would have broken, and
  // it is the cell that keeps @FR-Col-Group's `at least one keyed member` honest.
  c4 = VV { a: [], b: [] };
  c4.a = [E10{k:1,n:"alpha"}, E10{k:2,n:"beta"}];
  assert(len(c4.a) == 2, "c4 first vector len = {len(c4.a)}, want 2");
  assert(len(c4.b) == 0, "c4 second vector len = {len(c4.b)}, want 0 — two plain vectors are independent");

  println("901 ok");
}

struct R1e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R1 { es: vector<R1e>, by_k: hash<R1e[k]> }
// ---- r2/r3/r4: nullable holder — null write, replace, and a record into a null slot --------
struct R2e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R2 { es: vector<R2e?>, by_k: hash<R2e[k]> }
// ---- r5/r6: remove(i) — then a re-add of the same key; the last index; out of range ---------
struct R5e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R5 { es: vector<R5e>, by_k: hash<R5e[k]> }
// ---- r7/r8: one nesting level down, remove and replace --------------------------------------
struct R7i { n: text, v: integer }
struct R7r { items: vector<R7i>, by_n: hash<R7i[n]> }
struct R7 { rooms: vector<R7r> }
// ---- r9: four members — a replace re-links hash, sorted and trie ----------------------------
struct R9e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R9 { es: vector<R9e>, by_k: hash<R9e[k]>, by_s: sorted<R9e[n]>, by_t: trie<R9e[n]> }
// ---- r10: replace with the SAME key ---------------------------------------------------------
struct R10e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R10 { es: vector<R10e>, by_k: hash<R10e[k]> }
// ---- r11: the index expression is evaluated ONCE --------------------------------------------
struct R11e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R11 { es: vector<R11e>, by_k: hash<R11e[k]> }
fn r11_first(log: &text) -> integer { log += "x"; 0 }
// ---- r12: remove in a loop -----------------------------------------------------------------
struct R12e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R12 { es: vector<R12e>, by_k: hash<R12e[k]> }
// ---- r13: the group lives in an enum VARIANT -----------------------------------------------
struct R13e { k: integer, n: text }
enum R13s { R13b { es: vector<R13e>, by_k: hash<R13e[k]> }, R13d { x: integer } }
// ---- r14: CONTROLS — an ungrouped vector's replace, null write and remove are unchanged ------
struct R14e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R14 { es: vector<R14e>, nes: vector<R14e?>, tick: integer }
// ---- r15: CONTROL — `e#remove` in a loop still maintains the group (loft#903) ---------------
struct R15e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R15 { es: vector<R15e>, by_k: hash<R15e[k]> }
// ---- r17: nested under a `vector<R?>` element -----------------------------------------------
struct R17i { n: text, v: integer }
struct R17r { items: vector<R17i>, by_n: hash<R17i[n]> }
struct R17 { rooms: vector<R17r?> }
// ---- r18: the write route is a PARAMETER -----------------------------------------------
struct R18e { k: integer, n: text }
struct R18 { es: vector<R18e>, by_k: hash<R18e[k]> }
fn r18_drop(w: R18) { w.es.remove(0); }
fn r18_put(w: R18) { w.es[0] = R18e { k: 5, n: "e" }; }

fn test_a_group_element_written_through_the_vector_member_reaches_every_member() {
  // r1
  w1 = R1 {}; w1.es += [R1e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R1e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w1.es[0] = R1e { k: 11, n: "c" };
  assert(len(w1.es) == 2 && len(w1.by_k) == 2, "r1 lens: es={len(w1.es)} by={len(w1.by_k)}");
  assert(w1.by_k[11].n == "c" && w1.by_k[7] == null && w1.by_k[9].n == "b",
    "r1 replace: new key findable, old key gone");
  w1.es[1] = w1.es[0];
  assert(len(w1.by_k) == 1 && w1.by_k[11].n == "c" && w1.by_k[9] == null,
    "r1 replace from a sibling element: the displaced key leaves, the copied key dedups to one");

  // r2 null write
  w2 = R2 {}; w2.es += [R2e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R2e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w2.es[0] = null;
  assert(len(w2.es) == 2 && len(w2.by_k) == 1 && w2.by_k[7] == null && w2.by_k[9].n == "b",
    "r2 null write: es={len(w2.es)} by={len(w2.by_k)}");
  // r3 replace a present slot
  w3 = R2 {}; w3.es += [R2e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R2e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w3.es[0] = R2e { k: 11, n: "c" };
  assert(len(w3.by_k) == 2 && w3.by_k[11].n == "c" && w3.by_k[7] == null,
    "r3 nullable holder replace: by={len(w3.by_k)}");
  e3 = R2e { k: 13, n: "d" };
  w3.es[1] = e3;
  assert(len(w3.by_k) == 2 && w3.by_k[13].n == "d" && w3.by_k[9] == null,
    "r3 replace from a local: by={len(w3.by_k)}");
  // r4 a record into a null slot
  w4 = R2 {}; w4.es += [null, R2e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w4.es[0] = R2e { k: 12, n: "d" };
  assert(len(w4.by_k) == 2 && w4.by_k[12].n == "d" && w4.by_k[9].n == "b",
    "r4 null slot gets a record: by={len(w4.by_k)}");

  // r5 remove then re-add the same key
  w5 = R5 {}; w5.es += [R5e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R5e { k: 9, n: "b" }, R5e { k: 5, n: "c" }];
  w5.es.remove(0);
  assert(len(w5.es) == 2 && len(w5.by_k) == 2 && w5.by_k[7] == null,
    "r5 remove: es={len(w5.es)} by={len(w5.by_k)}");
  w5.by_k += [R5e { k: 7, n: "a2" }];
  assert(len(w5.es) == 3 && len(w5.by_k) == 3 && w5.by_k[7].n == "a2",
    "r5 re-add after remove: es={len(w5.es)} by={len(w5.by_k)}");
  // r6 the last index, the boolean, out of range
  ok = w5.es.remove(2);
  oob = w5.es.remove(7);
  assert(ok && !oob && len(w5.es) == 2 && len(w5.by_k) == 2 && w5.by_k[7] == null,
    "r6 remove last: ok={ok} oob={oob} es={len(w5.es)} by={len(w5.by_k)}");

  // r7/r8 nested
  w7 = R7 {}; w7.rooms += [R7r {}];
  w7.rooms[0].items += [R7i { n: "key", v: 3 }, R7i { n: "two", v: 4 }];
  w7.rooms[0].items.remove(0);
  assert(len(w7.rooms[0].items) == 1 && len(w7.rooms[0].by_n) == 1 && w7.rooms[0].by_n["key"] == null,
    "r7 nested remove: items={len(w7.rooms[0].items)} by={len(w7.rooms[0].by_n)}");
  w7.rooms[0].items[0] = R7i { n: "new", v: 9 };
  assert(len(w7.rooms[0].by_n) == 1 && w7.rooms[0].by_n["new"].v == 9 && w7.rooms[0].by_n["two"] == null,
    "r8 nested replace: by={len(w7.rooms[0].by_n)}");

  // r9 four members
  w9 = R9 {}; w9.es += [R9e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R9e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w9.es[0] = R9e { k: 11, n: "c" };
  assert(len(w9.by_k) == 2 && len(w9.by_s) == 2 && len(w9.by_t) == 2,
    "r9 lens: k={len(w9.by_k)} s={len(w9.by_s)} t={len(w9.by_t)}");
  assert(w9.by_k[11].n == "c" && w9.by_s["c"].k == 11 && w9.by_t["c"].k == 11
      && w9.by_k[7] == null && w9.by_s["a"] == null && w9.by_t["a"] == null,
    "r9 every keyed kind re-linked under the new key and rid of the old");
  w9.es.remove(1);
  assert(len(w9.by_k) == 1 && len(w9.by_s) == 1 && len(w9.by_t) == 1 && w9.by_t["b"] == null,
    "r9 remove leaves every keyed kind");

  // r10 same key
  w10 = R10 {}; w10.es += [R10e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R10e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w10.es[0] = R10e { k: 7, n: "a2" };
  assert(len(w10.es) == 2 && len(w10.by_k) == 2 && w10.by_k[7].n == "a2",
    "r10 same-key replace: es={len(w10.es)} by={len(w10.by_k)} n={w10.by_k[7].n}");

  // r11 index once
  w11 = R11 {}; w11.es += [R11e { k: 7, n: "a" }];
  log = "";
  w11.es[r11_first(log)] = R11e { k: 8, n: "z" };
  assert(len(log) == 1 && w11.by_k[8].n == "z" && w11.by_k[7] == null,
    "r11 index evaluated once: calls={len(log)}");

  // r12 remove in a loop
  w12 = R12 {}; w12.es += [R12e { k: 1, n: "a" }, R12e { k: 2, n: "b" }, R12e { k: 3, n: "c" }];
  for i in 0..2 { w12.es.remove(0); }
  assert(len(w12.es) == 1 && len(w12.by_k) == 1 && w12.by_k[3].n == "c" && w12.by_k[1] == null,
    "r12 loop remove: es={len(w12.es)} by={len(w12.by_k)}");

  // r13 variant
  v13: R13b = R13b { es: [], by_k: [] };
  v13.es += [R13e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R13e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  v13.es[0] = R13e { k: 11, n: "c" };
  assert(len(v13.by_k) == 2 && v13.by_k[11].n == "c" && v13.by_k[7] == null,
    "r13 variant replace: by={len(v13.by_k)}");
  v13.es.remove(1);
  assert(len(v13.by_k) == 1 && v13.by_k[9] == null, "r13 variant remove: by={len(v13.by_k)}");

  // r14 controls
  w14 = R14 {}; w14.es += [R14e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R14e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w14.nes += [R14e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R14e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  w14.es[0] = R14e { k: 11, n: "c" };
  w14.nes[0] = null;
  ok14 = w14.es.remove(1);
  assert(ok14 && len(w14.es) == 1 && w14.es[0].k == 11 && len(w14.nes) == 2 && w14.nes[0] == null && w14.nes[1].n == "b",
    "r14 ungrouped: es={len(w14.es)} nes={len(w14.nes)}");

  // r15 control: e#remove
  w15 = R15 {}; w15.es += [R15e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R15e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  for e in w15.es if e.k == 7 { e#remove; }
  assert(len(w15.es) == 1 && len(w15.by_k) == 1 && w15.by_k[7] == null, "r15 e#remove: by={len(w15.by_k)}");

  // r17 nested under vector<R?>
  w17 = R17 {}; w17.rooms += [R17r {}];
  w17.rooms[0].items += [R17i { n: "key", v: 3 }, R17i { n: "two", v: 4 }];
  w17.rooms[0].items.remove(0);
  // `(N-Chain)` — `w17.rooms[0]` is an index into a vector, so every member reached through it
  // is `τ?` and a READ of one asks for the discharge.  The writes above need none: an
  // assignment target and a mutating method's receiver are places `(N-Chain-Place)`.
  assert(len(w17.rooms[0].by_n ?? []) == 1 && w17.rooms[0].by_n["key"] == null,
    "r17 nested-nullable remove: by={len(w17.rooms[0].by_n ?? [])}");
  w17.rooms[0].items[0] = R17i { n: "new", v: 9 };
  assert(len(w17.rooms[0].by_n ?? []) == 1 && w17.rooms[0].by_n["new"].v == 9 && w17.rooms[0].by_n["two"] == null,
    "r17 nested-nullable replace: by={len(w17.rooms[0].by_n ?? [])}");

  // r18 & parameter
  w18 = R18 {}; w18.es += [R18e { k: 7, n: "a" }, R18e { k: 9, n: "b" }];
  r18_drop(w18);
  assert(len(w18.es) == 1 && len(w18.by_k) == 1 && w18.by_k[7] == null, "r18 remove via &: by={len(w18.by_k)}");
  r18_put(w18);
  assert(len(w18.by_k) == 1 && w18.by_k[5].n == "e" && w18.by_k[9] == null, "r18 replace via &: by={len(w18.by_k)}");

  // r19: an out-of-range index names no element — the write is dropped, the group untouched,
  // and the re-link after it must not touch a store the null place does not name.
  w18.es[9] = R18e { k: 1, n: "oob" };
  oob18 = w18.es.remove(9);
  assert(!oob18 && len(w18.es) == 1 && len(w18.by_k) == 1 && w18.by_k[1] == null && w18.by_k[5].n == "e",
    "r19 out-of-range write and remove: es={len(w18.es)} by={len(w18.by_k)}");
  println("ok");
}
